<article class="env-lemma" id="05QR">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/05QR">Lemma <span data-tag="05QR">13.4.9</span>.</a> Let $\mathcal{D}$ be a pre-triangulated category. Let $f : X \to Y$ be a morphism of $\mathcal{D}$. The following are equivalent </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
$f$ is an isomorphism, 
</p>
</li><li><p>
$(X, Y, 0, f, 0, 0)$ is a distinguished triangle, and 
</p>
</li><li><p>
for any distinguished triangle $(X, Y, Z, f, g, h)$ we have $Z = 0$. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      By TR1 the triangle $(X, X, 0, 1, 0, 0)$ is distinguished. Let $(X, Y, Z, f, g, h)$ be a distinguished triangle. By TR3 there is a map of distinguished triangles $(1, f, 0) : (X, X, 0) \to (X, Y, Z)$. If $f$ is an isomorphism, then $(1, f, 0)$ is an isomorphism of triangles by Lemma <a href="/tag/014A" data-tag="014A">13.4.3</a> and $Z = 0$. Conversely, if $Z = 0$, then $(1, f, 0)$ is an isomorphism of triangles as well, hence $f$ is an isomorphism. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>